Ethylene cracking furnaces are core components of steam-cracking facilities, applying high-temperature heat to hydrocarbon feedstocks such as ethane, naphtha, and liquefied petroleum gas to produce ethylene and other valuable olefins. These products are essential feedstocks for plastics, synthetic rubber, chemicals, and numerous industrial materials.

Demand is influenced by new petrochemical projects and modernization of existing facilities. Operators are increasingly evaluating energy efficiency, feedstock flexibility, reliability, emissions performance, and lifecycle operating costs when selecting furnace technologies.

